# The lock screen's security key check (FIDO2/U2F, pam_u2f), used when the system has no
# /etc/pam.d/h2s-shell-u2f (and not on NixOS, where a module named without a path is
# looked for only in libpam's own directory: the NixOS module writes the system-wide one).
# Offered when the user has a key in ~/.config/Yubico/u2f_keys ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/Yubico/
# u2f_keys), where pam_u2f looks without `authfile=`. Register the first key with
# `pamu2fcfg > ~/.config/Yubico/u2f_keys`, each further one with
# `pamu2fcfg -n >> ~/.config/Yubico/u2f_keys` (`>` on a file with keys throws them away).
#
# A passive check: it must never ask for typed input. The shell treats a passive check
# that asks as broken. So `cue` (pam_u2f says "touch the key", which needs no answer), and
# never `interactive` (it waits for Enter) — nor a key that needs a PIN (`pinverification`).
#
# Quickshell runs pam_authenticate and nothing else: only `auth` lines mean anything.
# The first line only says that PAM got going (pam_echo's message, never shown, changes no
# result): libpam loads every module before it runs, and a check that has not said it
# within a few seconds is stuck before any module ran, and is started again. Keep it first.
auth     [default=ignore] pam_echo.so h2s-shell-lock.started
auth     required pam_u2f.so cue
